content.text:
Good catch — the problem is real. Let me diagnose, then recommend.

## Why it fails

Current rule on the eyebrow:

```scss
.hero--with-image & {
    color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--app-selection-color) 70%, var(--app-eyebrow-color) 30%);
    text-shadow: 0 1px 6px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.4);
}
```

Two problems, both visible in your second screenshot:

1. **A blue-grey colour mix on a pale/bright backdrop is low-contrast by construction.** The backdrop can be anything — a white poster, a black movie frame, a red corporate logo. No single colour picks a winning contrast ratio against all of them.
2. **The black `text-shadow` is designed for dark backgrounds.** On a pale backdrop it looks like a dirty smudge and adds almost no contrast lift because the background isn't dark enough to "push" the text forward.

Theme-based detection (`prefers-color-scheme` or `.dark-theme`) won't fix this — a light-mode user can still be looking at a dark-blue backdrop, and vice versa.

## Options (sc-brainstorm)

**A — Remove it entirely.** The CTA button says "▶ Continue Watching" already. The dashboard rail directly below is literally "Recently Watched". The eyebrow is fully redundant context.
*Pro:* zero readability bug, cleaner hero. *Con:* a tiny labelling cue some users appreciate.

**B — Convert it to a self-contained chip** (dark-glass pill, white text, backdrop-blur). Always high contrast regardless of backdrop brightness. Netflix / Apple TV+ pattern — the little "N SERIES" or "Apple TV+ ORIGINAL" tag.
*Pro:* universal contrast, matches your existing chevron/see-all language, adds a streaming-app "stamp" feel. *Con:* adds a UI element; slightly busier hero.

**C — Keep it, but give it a local scrim.** A tiny radial vignette behind just the eyebrow text. Darker on dark theme, lighter on light theme — same approach as the hero's big vignette layer.
*Pro:* minimal visual change. *Con:* fiddly — scrim sizing depends on text length; fragile across languages; still theme-agnostic vs backdrop-agnostic.

**D — Move the label outside the image area.** Small label above the hero, like a section header ("Continue where you left off"). Keeps the hero image clean.
*Pro:* clean hero, no contrast challenge. *Con:* adds vertical space, splits the hero affordance away from its label.

## My vote: **B — chip it**, or if you lean minimal, **A — kill it**

I'd go with **B (chip)**. Reasoning:

- **Backdrop-agnostic**: A dark-glass pill with `backdrop-filter: blur(8px)` works against any photography. You already use this exact visual vocabulary on the rail chevrons — the eyebrow chip would feel like part of the same language.
- **Preserves hierarchy**: The uppercase tracked label still acts as a contextual eyebrow for the title. It signals "this is not just a movie poster — it's your resume point" in a way the CTA button can't quite do.
- **Streaming-app idiom**: Netflix's "CONTINUE WATCHING", "N SERIES", Apple TV+ "UP NEXT" — all rendered as chips or tags, never as naked typography over the backdrop.
- **Proven precedent**: This exact problem was solved by the streaming industry a decade ago and the answer was always "chip it". No need to reinvent.

If you prefer the minimal aesthetic (more Apple TV+ than Netflix), option **A** is also defensible — the redundancy with the CTA button is real. You'd lose a bit of context but gain cleaner composition.

Between B and A, I lean B because the dashboard is the very first screen — a bit of explicit labelling helps new users understand why this card is special, not just "a random poster".

## Concrete chip spec (if B)

```scss
.hero__eyebrow {
    align-self: flex-start;
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 4px;
    padding: 4px 10px;
    border-radius: 999px;
    background: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.55);
    backdrop-filter: blur(8px);
    -webkit-backdrop-filter: blur(8px);
    border: 1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.14);
    font-size: 0.68rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    line-height: 1;
    letter-spacing: 0.14em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: #fff;
    // No text-shadow — the chip itself handles contrast.
}

// Drop the theme-dependent overrides entirely — one rule, every theme,
// every backdrop.
```

Same background treatment as the chevrons → visual consistency across the dashboard language.

Want me to ship **B** (chip), **A** (remove), or a combination (B on hero, A on stalker-style plain-colour panels)? I'd go straight to B unless you have a reason to prefer something else.

